window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-3df854e3f30678ea"]={"key":"ny_b1-3df854e3f30678ea","group":"ny_b1","uid":"3df854e3f30678ea","rank":50,"title":"marlowe quicklane negotiation position","occupation":"customer_service_representatives","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Tobenna Aponte, the customer service representative preparing the internal negotiation brief for Marlowe Benefit Administrators. Marlowe’s chat platform was unavailable for eleven hours during Quicklane Auto Group’s open-enrolment period, and Quicklane’s general counsel has invoked the SLA penalty clause and asked to reopen the service terms. Ingrid Lindqvist, Marlowe’s VP Legal, needs a position she can use before Wednesday’s call, while Gerhard Bergqvist, Hilde Jaramillo, Joaquin Arriaga, Renata Coutinho, and Ibrahim Bruns will need to understand what can be offered and what still requires legal judgment. Use the supplied source file `quicklane_service_evidence.docx` as the factual record; do not add facts that are not supported there. Prepare `marlowe_quicklane_negotiation_brief.docx` and write it into the top level of the working directory the solver starts in, using exactly that filename. The document is for cross-functional internal stakeholders, not for sending to Quicklane, and must be no more than two pages. Make it a negotiation position and concession-sequence brief rather than a retelling of the outage. 1) Set out one clear opening position for Marlowe, including the commercial or operational terms Marlowe should put forward first and the rationale tied to the evidence. 2) Give a sequence of concessions that can be offered only in an intentional order if Quicklane pushes back; distinguish what is available early, what should be held back, and what would require a fresh decision. The dedicated enrolment line is inexpensive for Marlowe but a hard average-handle-time commitment and continuing SLA credits would create materially greater exposure, so the brief needs to make a defensible tradeoff rather than treating every remedy as equivalent. 3) State exactly one term that Marlowe will not move on, and explain why that boundary protects the account without pretending the outage did not matter. 4) Compare the relevant service periods in the source and explain the material change, including the limits of what the evidence can establish. 5) Mark the points where Ingrid Lindqvist must decide or confirm a contract interpretation, waiver, penalty entitlement, release, or revised legal obligation; do not make those calls on her behalf. 6) Separate a firm negotiating commitment from an internal service target or aspiration, and make clear which statements are safe to carry into a live discussion. 7) Include a compact risk-and-owner view so that an approver can see the commercial, operational, and legal consequence of each major position and who must be consulted before it is offered. 8) End with the single recommendation Marlowe should take into Wednesday’s call, not a list of open-ended next steps. Keep the tone candid and practical: acknowledge Quicklane’s disruption, avoid adversarial language, preserve room to negotiate, and do not include system instructions, a transcript, invented financial figures, or a draft contract. The completed file must stand on its own for a stakeholder who has not attended the internal preparation meeting.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/quicklane_service_evidence.docx` — Factual evidence bundle for the Quicklane outage, service-period comparison, current SLA wording, operational remedies, and internal constraints used to prepare the negotiation brief.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `marlowe_quicklane_negotiation_brief.docx` — A concise internal negotiation position and concession-sequence brief for Ingrid Lindqvist and Marlowe’s cross-functional stakeholders. (Write to the top level of the starting working directory.; Maximum two pages.; Must be usable without access to the author or an oral briefing.; Must contain one opening position, an ordered concession sequence, one immovable term, legal decision boundaries, period comparison, and a final recommendation.; Do not invent unsupported facts or make contract-interpretation decisions reserved for Legal.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/quicklane_service_evidence.docx","bytes":38444,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/a7fa89c73659886ef45902f9b3e2e1e9d9cc46cfe3f4ccbbf6ee34beef20cb71.docx","sha256":"a7fa89c73659886ef45902f9b3e2e1e9d9cc46cfe3f4ccbbf6ee34beef20cb71","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Quicklane Service Evidence\nMarlowe Benefit Administrators | Internal case record | 20 August 2026\nSERVICE SUMMARY\nQuicklane Auto Group reported that Marlowe’s chat support was unavailable for 11 hours during Quicklane’s open-enrolment period. The disruption affected employee enrolment support and triggered a formal customer complaint. This bundle records the available service evidence and internal constraints; it is not a legal determination.\nCOMPARABLE SERVICE PERIODS\nMeasure\nStable pre-incident period\nIncident-period month\nChat availability\n99.93%\n98.41%\nChat volume\n4,820\n5,460\nAbandonment\n2.1%\n14.8%\nMedian first response\n42 sec\n3 min 55 sec\nAverage handle time\n6 min 10 sec\n8 min 40 sec\nThe incident month shows lower availability and materially worse queue outcomes while volume was higher. The comparison does not establish sole causation, contractual entitlement, or future performance; it is a period-level operational signal.\nCURRENT SLA SCHEDULE (EXCERPT)\nThe current schedule sets a monthly chat-availability objective of 99.9%. If measured availability falls below 99.5%, the customer may request a service credit under the schedule, subject to the schedule’s notice, measurement, exclusions, and validation conditions. The schedule describes credits as the service-level remedy; whether this incident satisfies every condition is for Legal to interpret. Quicklane has invoked the SLA penalty clause and asked to reopen the service terms.\nOPERATIONAL OPTIONS\nOperations can staff a dedicated enrolment line for the next enrolment window at relatively low incremental cost; it is a controllable support measure, not a change to the SLA.\nA hard average-handle-time commitment would constrain case handling and require operational governance; Operations can propose controls but cannot promise a binding AHT outcome.\nStanding SLA credits would create recurring commercial exposure and are materially more consequential than the dedicated line.\nINTERNAL OWNERSHIP AND CONSTRAINTS\nIngrid Lindqvist (VP Legal) owns contract interpretation, waiver, release, amendment, and any revised legal obligation. Tobenna Aponte prepares the customer-service position. Gerhard Bergqvist and Hilde Jaramillo support operational design; Joaquin Arriaga and Renata Coutinho support account/commercial coordination; Ibrahim Bruns supports service execution. Account or customer service may frame an offer but cannot independently approve a permanent commercial obligation.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/marlowe_quicklane_negotiation_brief.docx","bytes":40144,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/16f1eeb7e59b36e927f4868d2519c6313b9804fe149cb8a648f0b4a3021f357b.docx","sha256":"16f1eeb7e59b36e927f4868d2519c6313b9804fe149cb8a648f0b4a3021f357b","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"MARLOWE × QUICKLANE\nNegotiation position brief\nFor Ingrid Lindqvist and cross-functional leads | Prepared by Tobenna Aponte | Before Wednesday’s call\nSituation: Chat support was unavailable for 11 hours during Quicklane’s open-enrolment period. Acknowledge the disruption and the formal complaint; do not concede that the SLA penalty is owed.\n1 | OPENING POSITION — ONE FIRST OFFER\nOffer a dedicated enrolment support line for Quicklane’s next enrolment window, with a jointly agreed operating plan and post-window review, while keeping the current SLA terms in place pending Legal’s review. This directly addresses the high-consequence enrolment disruption and is a relatively low-cost, controllable accommodation. It avoids turning a single incident into a permanent service or credit obligation. Safe to carry: “Marlowe can put a dedicated enrolment line and operating plan on the table.” Not safe to carry: any promise of credits, AHT, waiver, release, or reopened terms.\n2 | CONCESSION SEQUENCE (ONLY IN ORDER)\n#\nMove if pressed\nGuardrail / status\nOwner before offer\n1\nDedicated enrolment line + named operating plan\nEarly, low incremental cost; limited to next enrolment window and review. Firm operational offer once Operations confirms staffing.\nGerhard Bergqvist / Hilde Jaramillo\n2\nTime-boxed service review and corrective-action readout\nShare period comparison and controls; no admission of SLA breach or entitlement. Internal target, not a new SLA.\nTobenna Aponte; Ibrahim Bruns\n3\nExplore a narrowly scoped AHT service target\nOnly as an internal aspiration or pilot with governance, exclusions, and no binding outcome. A hard AHT commitment is materially higher exposure.\nOperations + Ingrid confirmation\n4\nAny credit, waiver, release, or reopened/permanent terms\nHold back. If Quicklane presses first for the penalty or permanent change, pause and route the exact text and conditions to Ingrid; no automatic standing credits.\nIngrid Lindqvist (Legal decision)\n3 | ONE IMMOVABLE TERM\nMarlowe will not agree to standing or continuing SLA credits as an automatic remedy. Quicklane’s service concern is legitimate, especially during open enrolment, but recurring credits create ongoing commercial exposure beyond the low-cost support line and should not be triggered by an internal negotiation brief. A case-specific credit, waiver, release, or revised obligation is a separate Legal decision—not a movement on this boundary.\n4 | EVIDENCE AND LEGAL BOUNDARIES\nPeriod comparison\nWhat Ingrid must decide / confirm\nStable pre-incident: availability 99.93%, volume 4,820, abandonment 2.1%, median first response 42 sec, AHT 6 min 10 sec. Incident month: 98.41%, 5,460, 14.8%, 3 min 55 sec, and 8 min 40 sec. Availability and queue outcomes deteriorated while volume rose. Evidence is period-level; it cannot prove sole causation, penalty entitlement, or future performance.\nQuicklane invoked the SLA penalty clause; that demand is not a conclusion that the clause is satisfied. Ingrid must confirm interpretation of the 99.5% credit trigger and notice/measurement/exclusion conditions, and approve any waiver, release, amendment, reopened terms, or revised legal obligation.\n5 | RISK / OWNER VIEW\nPosition\nConsequence if offered\nRisk lens\nConsult / owner\nDedicated line\nAddresses enrolment harm; manageable staffing lift.\nOperational / low commercial\nGerhard, Hilde, Ibrahim\nAHT target or commitment\nMay constrain case handling; binding promise could create service exposure.\nOperational / legal\nOperations + Ingrid\nStanding credits / reopened terms\nRecurring cost and precedent; may alter rights.\nCommercial / legal high\nIngrid; Joaquin / Renata\nPeriod comparison + review\nCredible accountability without admitting liability.\nReputational / operational\nTobenna; Ibrahim\nRECOMMENDATION FOR WEDNESDAY\nTake one controlled offer: acknowledge the 11-hour open-enrolment disruption, offer the dedicated enrolment line and time-boxed review, and hold all credits, AHT commitments, waivers, releases, and permanent term changes for Ingrid’s decision. This preserves a practical remedy without converting an unresolved SLA question into a continuing obligation.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies the eleven-hour chat-platform outage during Quicklane’s open-enrolment period.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening context or situation statement.","grader_hint":"Confirm that both the duration and open-enrolment context appear accurately."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief accurately compares the service periods identified in the source and explains the material change rather than merely listing two values.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period-comparison passage or table.","grader_hint":"Compare every stated period figure with the source and require an explicit interpretation of the change."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The opening position contains exactly one coherent first offer rather than several mutually competing alternatives.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clearly labelled opening position.","grader_hint":"A reader should be able to quote one initial position without choosing among options."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The opening position addresses the outage’s customer impact while preserving Marlowe’s ability to avoid an unnecessarily permanent commercial obligation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening-position rationale.","grader_hint":"Look for a balanced remedy and rationale, not a refusal or an unlimited make-good."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The dedicated enrolment line appears as an early, low-cost concession or offer in the sequence.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession sequence.","grader_hint":"Confirm that it is not buried after high-cost or legally consequential concessions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence preserves the distinction between operational support that is relatively inexpensive and commitments that create ongoing exposure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ordered concessions and rationale.","grader_hint":"The ordering and explanation must show economic or risk prioritization."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A hard average-handle-time commitment is treated as a materially more consequential concession than a service-support measure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession treatment of AHT.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the brief explains the operational risk or conditions attached to any AHT discussion."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Continuing or standing SLA credits are not offered as an automatic early concession and are held for an appropriately higher approval threshold or rejected as the red line.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Sequence and red-line sections.","grader_hint":"The document must demonstrate deliberate sequencing, not simply mention SLA credits."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The brief states exactly one immovable term in language that a stakeholder could identify without interpretation.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Explicit boundary or red-line statement.","grader_hint":"Do not award if the document lists several red lines or uses only vague language such as 'protect our interests'."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The immovable term is justified by account protection, controllability, or exposure evidenced in the source, while still acknowledging Quicklane’s legitimate service concern.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Red-line rationale.","grader_hint":"Require both a defensible business reason and a non-dismissive treatment of Quicklane."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The concession sequence is ordered from lower-cost or lower-risk accommodation toward higher-cost or higher-obligation measures.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Numbered or otherwise unambiguous sequence.","grader_hint":"Check order, not just inclusion of concessions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each major concession has a stated condition, limit, or approval gate that prevents it from becoming an unconditional promise.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concession entries.","grader_hint":"At least the substantive offers must have practical guardrails."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief identifies a point at which Ingrid Lindqvist’s decision or confirmation is required for contract interpretation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal-boundary language.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit Legal decision marker, not merely the name Ingrid Lindqvist."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes the fact that Quicklane invoked the SLA penalty clause from a conclusion that the penalty is legally owed.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"SLA discussion.","grader_hint":"The wording must preserve the difference between a demand, a contractual text, and a legal determination."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any discussion of waiver, release, amendment, or reopened terms is expressly presented as requiring Legal review or decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal-review markers.","grader_hint":"Credit only when the boundary is attached to the relevant issue."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief does not pre-empt Ingrid Lindqvist by declaring the enforceability, interpretation, or final remedy under the SLA.","weight":-5.0,"polarity":"penalty","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"Apply the penalty for categorical legal conclusions stated as settled fact rather than a question or approval gate."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief separates firm negotiating commitments from internal service targets, aspirations, or exploratory options.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Wording of offers and operational measures.","grader_hint":"Check whether a stakeholder could tell what may safely be said externally."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies which statements are safe to carry into the live discussion and which are internal-only or subject to approval.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commitment-status labels or equivalent prose.","grader_hint":"The distinction must be operationally usable, not implied solely by tone."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief includes risks or consequences for the operational, commercial, and legal dimensions of the position.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk-and-owner view.","grader_hint":"Require all three dimensions, with consequences tied to actual recommendations."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The major positions identify an accountable owner or required stakeholder for action or approval.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Owner column, labels, or explicit owner language.","grader_hint":"Names may be used where supported by the request; do not require invented departments or titles."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief explains what Marlowe should do if Quicklane presses first for the penalty or for a permanent service-term change.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pushback handling in the sequence.","grader_hint":"Look for a controlled response path rather than a generic statement to negotiate."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation accounts for the fact that the incident occurred during a high-consequence enrolment window.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rationale or risk section.","grader_hint":"The timing must affect the recommendation or remedy, not be repeated decoratively."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document avoids unsupported financial figures, service metrics, or promises and uses only source-supported numbers.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quantitative and factual claims.","grader_hint":"Cross-check every number and concrete factual assertion against the supplied evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief makes clear what the supplied evidence cannot establish.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations or qualification language.","grader_hint":"Credit for meaningful evidentiary limits, especially around causation, entitlement, or future performance."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The prose is suitable for cross-functional internal stakeholders: candid, commercially practical, and neither adversarial nor evasive.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"Judge whether Legal, operations, and account stakeholders could use it in preparation without rewriting its tone."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A stakeholder who did not attend the preparation meeting can understand the position, sequence, boundaries, and recommendation without asking the author to decode it.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"Assess standalone usability, explicit dependencies, and absence of unexplained shorthand."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The brief ends with one clear recommendation for Wednesday’s call rather than a menu of unresolved alternatives or a next-steps list.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph or closing recommendation.","grader_hint":"There must be one call to take, and the ending must not defer the entire decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The final recommendation is consistent with the opening position, concession order, and immovable term.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-check of all substantive sections.","grader_hint":"Mark down contradictions such as recommending the red line, reversing the sequence, or changing the offer."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered document is no more than two pages.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX pagination.","grader_hint":"Render the file with a standard office-compatible renderer and count pages; do not accept microscopic or illegible text as a workaround."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is a readable DOCX with the exact required filename and contains the negotiation brief content.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Filesystem and document inspection.","grader_hint":"Verify exact top-level filename, successful opening, and non-empty readable text."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an internal customer-case communication prepared by a finance-and-insurance customer service representative for Legal and cross-functional leaders before a live account negotiation. The artifact must convert a supplied evidence bundle into a controlled, approvable position rather than merely summarize the incident.","difficulty_sources":["Reconcile a mixed-format evidence bundle into a concise, source-faithful position.","Model a concession sequence under competing customer, operational, and commercial pressures.","Synthesize a single recommendation while marking Legal's decision boundary and separating musts from shoulds."],"authoring_plan":{"source_file":{"filename":"quicklane_service_evidence.docx","construction":"Create one compact evidence bundle in DOCX containing a service summary, a short period-comparison table, an excerpted current SLA schedule, an operations note, and an internal finance/legal note. It should feel like one assembled workplace source document rather than a rulebook. Keep all facts mutually consistent.","facts":{"incident":"Chat support was unavailable for 11 hours during Quicklane Auto Group's open-enrolment period. The disruption affected employee enrolment support and triggered a formal customer complaint.","period_comparison":"The source should provide two comparable service periods: a stable pre-incident period and the incident-period month. Include supported values for availability, chat volume, abandonment, and response or handling performance, with the incident period showing the material deterioration. Do not make the answer depend on a single literal cell.","current_terms":"The current SLA should state the existing service-level framework and the penalty-credit mechanism, while leaving the interpretation of whether this incident satisfies every contractual condition to Legal. Include enough wording for a professional to distinguish existing text from Quicklane's requested reopened terms.","operational_options":"Operations can support a dedicated enrolment line for the next enrolment window at relatively low incremental cost. A hard average-handle-time commitment would constrain case handling and require operational governance. Standing SLA credits would create recurring commercial exposure and should be treated as materially more consequential than the dedicated line.","internal_constraints":"The source should say that Legal owns contract interpretation, waiver, release, and amendment decisions; operations can propose service controls but cannot promise a binding AHT outcome; account or customer service can frame the offer but cannot independently approve a permanent commercial obligation.","stakeholders":"Use the named people from the request in plausible roles: Ingrid Lindqvist as VP Legal and decision owner for legal interpretation, with Tobenna Aponte preparing the brief and the other named people representing relevant internal consultation or execution roles. Do not introduce additional personal names."}},"gold_derivation":"Build a reference solution that extracts only supported facts, compares the two source periods, proposes the dedicated enrolment line as an early accommodation, holds higher-exposure AHT and standing-credit commitments behind conditions or approval, identifies exactly one defensible red line, and labels Legal decision points without deciding them. The reference must include an ordered concession sequence, owner/risk treatment, commitment-versus-target distinction, limitations, and one final recommendation. Render it under two pages with readable office-document formatting.","reachability_check":"All factual values are supplied in the single source file; all remaining rubric expectations are ordinary negotiation-position, customer-service, operational-governance, and legal-boundary practices reachable by a competent practitioner. No private code, hidden template, or live information is required."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"supplied_template"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"quicklane_service_evidence","filename":"quicklane_service_evidence.docx","purpose":"Factual evidence bundle for the Quicklane outage, service-period comparison, current SLA wording, operational remedies, and internal constraints used to prepare the negotiation brief.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this file before drafting; it is the only supplied source of case facts."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"negotiation_position_brief","filename":"marlowe_quicklane_negotiation_brief.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A concise internal negotiation position and concession-sequence brief for Ingrid Lindqvist and Marlowe’s cross-functional stakeholders.","required":true,"constraints":["Write to the top level of the starting working directory.","Maximum two pages.","Must be usable without access to the author or an oral briefing.","Must contain one opening position, an ordered concession sequence, one immovable term, legal decision boundaries, period comparison, and a final recommendation.","Do not invent unsupported facts or make contract-interpretation decisions reserved for Legal."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"customer_case_response","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"customer_service_representatives","sector":"finance_insurance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":2.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have an independent domain reviewer inspect the rendered brief against every rubric item, with a second reviewer checking legal-boundary overreach, unsupported claims, and internal consistency. Use source comparison for factual items and professional judgment for usability and tone.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The exact commercial figures and period metrics are intentionally source-dependent; grading should use relational agreement with the supplied evidence rather than fixed cell answers.","The identity of the single red-line term may be expressed in different defensible wording, but it must be singular, explicit, and consistent with the evidence and sequence."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:09:50+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:09:50+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:09:50+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:09:50+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:09:50+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/3df854e3f30678ea","declared_deliverables":["marlowe_quicklane_negotiation_brief.docx"]};
